#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f2b34d4c-905e-56b4-94eb-04d177c1c9d4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220208} {:answer-field/id #uuid "2d759da5-300e-5cf3-941a-a273a1c633d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b10f5340-8ef5-5fb3-b26f-d1f92aac8ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-329001" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3$, and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| $e^{At} = e^{3t}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2t$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $2t + 1$ |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7694b518-7142-5675-a89b-c808f56e6519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7694b518-7142-5675-a89b-c808f56e6519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306281} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d6fa421-5689-5aa3-8796-026376a17eaf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6300be7f-13fc-51f9-a766-c305037c844a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6300be7f-13fc-51f9-a766-c305037c844a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}(5t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306109} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8a68fda-03fb-510b-ad4a-29ed331aec09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b84514fc-4c30-5d7a-9804-4a0f60502965" :question/math-academy-id "q-334631" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -10{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+5\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-5\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1} &=\\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f9442c5d-278f-5ccc-aa2c-1144a132ac76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220687} {:answer-field/id #uuid "25918028-a170-523b-9cee-33d9dd6c7b4c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "092bcce6-22aa-54b4-8fc0-6de76116f21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334632" :question/problem "Find $e^{At}$ for $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $± \\textrm{i}$.*\n\n| $e^{At} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | $\\sin (t)$ |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\cos (t) - \\sin (t)$ |  |  |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19077b39-5510-509d-9a89-ee45f6b5d61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19077b39-5510-509d-9a89-ee45f6b5d61e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305379} :answer-field/id #uuid "3638313c-e8b7-5c36-8156-7411f434b4d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307693} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9392ac7-2fb2-5f27-871a-3472eaf12ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9392ac7-2fb2-5f27-871a-3472eaf12ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306745} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3239541-2877-5189-9f0d-345779dbab9f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05e36fef-5d87-510d-a2e4-12cce4dfd1e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334734" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $6,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{6t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1+t$ | $t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=6t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}r(6t) &={e}^{6t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(6t) &={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0} &={e}^{6t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1} &={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{6t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n6t{e}^{6t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}(1-6t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-{e}^{6t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(-t), \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{6t}t+{e}^{6t}(1-6t) \\\\  & ={e}^{6t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1+t & t \\\\ -t & 1-t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221411 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733a3974-987f-5080-805d-a66efabda43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "733a3974-987f-5080-805d-a66efabda43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575160} :answer-field/id #uuid "25fcf268-273b-59de-a67a-190bf168f7df" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575428} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4c7b80-1a98-51b7-9af3-e76dbb84be64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e4c7b80-1a98-51b7-9af3-e76dbb84be64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574996} :answer-field/id #uuid "425822a2-148d-5a7c-937d-bc2f307f5c40" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7af5a848-8e99-5e88-9522-12198357b909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-3t)e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7af5a848-8e99-5e88-9522-12198357b909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-3t)e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575172} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a85e532-8708-5354-86f1-6ed86ff86ac4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f98c6d7-cccf-5a2f-9c72-b538fb6ef929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f98c6d7-cccf-5a2f-9c72-b538fb6ef929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575151} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d67750-f402-5452-9530-c1f22787272d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f681488-76a6-582b-8440-bda618691a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-335091" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{-3t} & (1-3t){e}^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
